If each VPS can connect directly to every managed database (DBaaS) instance, you can run SymmetricDS in a "Hub-and-Spoke" or "Multi-Master Mesh" architecture using standard JDBC connections—completely eliminating S3 buckets, custom shell scripts, and cross-VPS HTTP communication.
In this setup, SymmetricDS acts as a standalone replication engine sitting alongside your databases. Because SymmetricDS already pools JDBC connections and groups transactions into scheduled batches, it solves your connection-overhead problem out of the box.

How This Architecture Works

1. Connection Pooling & Batching: SymmetricDS keeps a small, persistent JDBC connection pool open to the databases it manages. Instead of connecting per transaction, it captures changes locally via database triggers into sym_data tables, bundles them into batches on a timer (e.g., every 10–30 seconds), and applies them to the remote databases over those pooled connections.
2. Two Deployment Patterns:
	• Single Central Engine (Simplest): You run SymmetricDS on one VPS (say, region-1), configured with 4 engine .properties files (one pointing to each regional DBaaS). All 4 engines run inside the same JVM process and sync with each other locally in memory. (If region-1 goes down, replication pauses until it recovers or fails over).
	• Distributed Engines (High Availability): You run one SymmetricDS instance on each of the 4 VPS machines. Each VPS connects locally to its own region's DBaaS over JDBC, and the 4 SymmetricDS nodes push/pull batches to each other over HTTPS (port 31415).
Below is a full, working example of the Distributed 4-Node Mesh (since you already have 4 VPS machines), where Region 1 is the registration server and Regions 1–4 sync bidirectionally.

Step 1: Firewall & Database Prep

1. Managed DB Firewall: Allow inbound connections on your DBaaS port (3306 for MySQL / 5432 for PostgreSQL) only from its local regional VPS IP (e.g., vps-1 -> dbaas-1), with SSL required.
2. VPS Firewall: Allow inbound TCP port 31415 (HTTPS) between the static IPs of vps-1, vps-2, vps-3, and vps-4.
3. Prevent Primary Key Collisions (Crucial for Multi-Master): If all 4 regions accept writes, configure auto-increment offsets on each DBaaS so IDs never collide:
	• MySQL: Set auto_increment_increment = 4 on all DBs, and auto_increment_offset = 1, 2, 3, 4 on DBs 1–4 respectively.
	• PostgreSQL: Alter sequences on each DB: ALTER SEQUENCE your_table_id_seq INCREMENT BY 4 RESTART WITH 1; (using 1, 2, 3, 4 per region).

Step 2: Configure SymmetricDS Engines on Each VPS

Install SymmetricDS on all 4 VPS machines (e.g., /opt/symmetric-ds). Place one .properties file inside /opt/symmetric-ds/engines/ on each VPS.

On vps-1 (/opt/symmetric-ds/engines/region-1.properties)

(Acts as the registration server that coordinates cluster configuration)
properties
engine.name=region-1
group.id=regional_cluster
external.id=1

# Direct JDBC connection to Region 1 DBaaS (MySQL Example)
db.driver=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver
db.url=jdbc:mysql://://example.com
db.user=sym_user
db.password=STRONG_DB_PASSWORD

# Registration & Sync URLs (Use private VPC IPs or public IPs locked by firewall)
registration.url=
sync.url=https://10.0.1

# Batching & Performance Tuning (Avoids connection overhead)
job.push.period.time.ms=10000
job.pull.period.time.ms=10000
db.pool.max.active=10
Use code with caution.

On vps-2 (/opt/symmetric-ds/engines/region-2.properties)

(Repeat for region-3 and region-4, incrementing external.id to 3 and 4)
properties
engine.name=region-2
group.id=regional_cluster
external.id=2

# Direct JDBC connection to Region 2 DBaaS (PostgreSQL Example)
db.driver=org.postgresql.Driver
db.url=jdbc:postgresql://://example.com
db.user=sym_user
db.password=STRONG_DB_PASSWORD

# Points to Region 1 to download replication rules on first boot
registration.url=https://10.0.1
sync.url=https://10.0.2

job.push.period.time.ms=10000
job.pull.period.time.ms=10000
db.pool.max.active=10
Use code with caution.
(Note: If you strictly want one VPS to connect directly to all 4 DBaaS instances without VPS-to-VPS HTTPS traffic, you simply put all 4 .properties files into /opt/symmetric-ds/engines/ on vps-1 and set their sync.url to http://localhost:31415/sync/region-X.)

Step 3: Define Replication Rules on Region 1

Start SymmetricDS on vps-1 first (/opt/symmetric-ds/bin/sym_service start). It will automatically create its sym_* metadata tables inside dbaas-region1.
Run the following SQL against dbaas-region1 (using Wintersys's ConnectToMySQLDB.sh or ConnectToPostgresDB.sh) to configure bidirectional sync across all nodes in regional_cluster:
sql
-- 1. Register the Node Group
INSERT INTO sym_node_group (node_group_id, description) 
VALUES ('regional_cluster', '4-Region Active-Active DB Cluster');

-- 2. Link the group to itself so all 4 regions sync with one another
-- 'P' = Push changes every 10s, 'W' = Wait for pull
INSERT INTO sym_node_group_link (source_node_group_id, target_node_group_id, data_event_action) 
VALUES ('regional_cluster', 'regional_cluster', 'P');

-- 3. Create a Channel (controls batch sizes and throughput)
INSERT INTO sym_channel (channel_id, processing_order, max_batch_size, max_batch_to_send, extract_period_millis, enabled) 
VALUES ('app_data', 1, 1000, 10, 0, 1);

-- 4. Define which tables to replicate (Wildcard '*' syncs all tables except sym_*)
INSERT INTO sym_trigger (trigger_id, source_table_name, channel_id, sync_on_incoming_batch, last_update_time, create_time) 
VALUES ('all_tables', '*', 'app_data', 0, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);
-- Note: sync_on_incoming_batch = 0 prevents infinite replication loops!

-- 5. Route changes from the trigger to the cluster
INSERT INTO sym_router (router_id, source_node_group_id, target_node_group_id, router_type, create_time, last_update_time) 
VALUES ('cluster_router', 'regional_cluster', 'regional_cluster', 'default', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);

-- 6. Bind the Trigger to the Router
INSERT INTO sym_trigger_router (trigger_id, router_id, initial_load_order, last_update_time, create_time) 
VALUES ('all_tables', 'cluster_router', 100, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);

-- 7. Open registration for Regions 2, 3, and 4
INSERT INTO sym_node (node_id, node_group_id, external_id, sync_enabled) VALUES ('2', 'regional_cluster', '2', 1);
INSERT INTO sym_node (node_id, node_group_id, external_id, sync_enabled) VALUES ('3', 'regional_cluster', '3', 1);
INSERT INTO sym_node (node_id, node_group_id, external_id, sync_enabled) VALUES ('4', 'regional_cluster', '4', 1);

INSERT INTO sym_node_security (node_id, node_password, registration_enabled) VALUES ('2', 'SHARED_CLUSTER_SECRET', 1);
INSERT INTO sym_node_security (node_id, node_password, registration_enabled) VALUES ('3', 'SHARED_CLUSTER_SECRET', 1);
INSERT INTO sym_node_security (node_id, node_password, registration_enabled) VALUES ('4', 'SHARED_CLUSTER_SECRET', 1);
Use code with caution.

Step 4: Start the Remaining Nodes

1. Start SymmetricDS on vps-2, vps-3, and vps-4:bash
/opt/symmetric-ds/bin/sym_service start
Use code with caution.
2. Each node will automatically contact region-1, download the triggers and routing rules, build its local sym_* tables on its respective DBaaS, and begin batch-syncing changes every 10 seconds.

Key Caveats to Watch For

• Conflict Resolution: If two users edit the exact same row in region-1 and region-2 within the same 10-second batch window, the last batch applied wins by default. You can add entries to sym_conflict (e.g., NEWER_WINS using a timestamp column) if concurrent edits to the same row are likely.
• Schema Changes (DDL): SymmetricDS replicates row data (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) automatically, not schema changes (ALTER TABLE). Apply schema migrations across all 4 DBaaS instances simultaneously during deployments, or use SymmetricDS's symadmin send-schema utility.
If you'd like to refine this setup, let me know:
• Whether you prefer the Single-VPS Multi-Engine model (zero VPS-to-VPS ports needed) or the 4-VPS Distributed model shown above
• If you have existing data that needs an initial load before live sync starts
• Whether you need conflict resolution rules for concurrent updates to the same rows
I can adjust the configuration and SQL scripts to match.
